"Sparrow: Space-Efficient zkSNARK for Data-Parallel Circuits and Applications to Zero-Knowledge Decision Trees (https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/1631)\nciteturn3view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/1631\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 73\nL0: # What a lovely hat\nL1: #### Is it made out of 【0†tin foil†iacr.org】?\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Cryptology ePrint Archive】\nL4: \nL5:   * 【2†Papers】 Updates from the last:\nL6:     * 【3†7 days】\nL7:     * 【4†31 days】\nL8:     * 【5†6 months】\nL9:     * 【6†365 days】\nL10:     * * * *\nL11: \nL12:     * 【7†Listing by year】\nL13:     * 【8†All papers】\nL14:     * 【9†Compact view】\nL15:     * 【10†Subscribe†www.iacr.org】\nL16:     * * * *\nL17: \nL18:     * 【11†How to cite】\nL19:     * * * *\nL20:     * 【12†Harvesting metadata】\nL21:   * 【2†Submissions】\nL22:     * 【13†Submit a paper】\nL23:     * 【14†Revise or withdraw a paper】\nL24:     * 【15†Acceptance and publishing conditions】\nL25: \nL26: 【16†Image: Search Button】\nL27: \nL28: [Input] [Button: Search ]\nL29: #### Paper 2024/1631\nL30: \nL31: ### 【17†Sparrow: Space-Efficient zkSNARK for Data-Parallel Circuits and Applications to Zero-Knowledge Decision Trees】\nL32: \nL33: Christodoulos Pappas, Hong Kong University of Science and Technology\nL34: \nL35: Dimitrios Papadopoulos, Hong Kong University of Science and Technology\nL36: ##### Abstract\nL37: Space-efficient SNARKs aim to reduce the prover's space overhead which is one the main obstacles for deploying SNARKs in practice, as it can be prohibitively large (e.g., orders of magnitude larger than natively performing the computation).\nL38: In this work, we propose Sparrow, a novel space-efficient zero-knowledge SNARK for data-parallel arithmetic circuits with two attractive features: (i) it is the first space-efficient scheme where, for a given field, the prover overhead increases with a multiplicative sublogarithmic factor as the circuit size increases, and (ii) compared to prior space-efficient SNARKs that work for arbitrary arithmetic circuits, it achieves prover space asymptotically smaller than the circuit size itself.\nL39: Our key building block is a novel space-efficient sumcheck argument with improved prover time which may be of independent interest. Our experimental results for three use cases (arbitrary data parallel circuits, multiplication trees, batch SHA256 hashing) indicate Sparrow outperforms the prior state-of-the-art space-efficient SNARK for arithmetic circuits Gemini (Bootle et al., EUROCRYPT'22) by $3.2$-$28.7\\times$ in total prover space and $3.1$-$11.3\\times$ in prover time.\nL40: We then use Sparrow to build zero-knowledge proofs of tree training and prediction, relying on its space efficiency to scale to large datasets and forests of multiple trees. Compared to a (non-space-efficient) optimal-time SNARK based on the GKR protocol, we observe prover space reduction of $16$-$240\\times$ for tree training while maintaining essentially the same prover and verifier times and proof size.\nL41: Even more interestingly, our prover requires comparable space to natively performing the underlying computation. E.g., for a $400$MB dataset, our prover only needs $1.4\\times$ more space than the native computation.\nL42: ##### Metadata\nL43: Available format(s)\nL44:     【17†PDF】\nL45: Category\nL46:     【18†Cryptographic protocols】\nL47: Publication info\nL48:     Published elsewhere. Major revision. ACM CCS 2024\nL49: Keywords\nL50:     【19†zkSNARKs】【20†space-efficient zkSNARKs】【21†zero-knowledge proofs】【22†proof of training】\nL51: Contact author(s)\nL52:     cpappas @ connect ust hk\nL53: dipapado @ cse ust hk\nL54: History\nL55:     2024-10-14: approved\nL56:     2024-10-11: received\nL57:     【23†See all versions】\nL58: Short URL\nL59:     【24†https://ia.cr/2024/1631†ia.cr】\nL60: License\nL61: \nL62: 【25†CC BY†creativecommons.org】\nL63: BibTeX 【26†Image】Copy to clipboard\nL64: \nL65:     @misc{cryptoeprint:2024/1631,\nL66:           author = {Christodoulos Pappas and Dimitrios Papadopoulos},\nL67:           title = {Sparrow: Space-Efficient {zkSNARK} for Data-Parallel Circuits and Applications to Zero-Knowledge Decision Trees},\nL68:           howpublished = {Cryptology {ePrint} Archive, Paper 2024/1631},\nL69:           year = {2024},\nL70:           url = {https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/1631}\nL71:     }\nL72: Note: In order to protect the privacy of readers, eprint.iacr.org does not use cookies or embedded third party content.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn3view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/ChristodoulosPappas/Sparrow-Full-Version\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://github.com/ChristodoulosPappas/Sparrow-Full-Version: Cache miss--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2610.14849] StateSync-GKR: Design and Evaluation of a Reusable Sparse GKR Engine (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14849)\nciteturn3view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14849\",\"lineno\":10}); Total lines: 166\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Cryptography and Security\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 10 Oct 2026 (【5†v1】), last revised 26 Oct 2026 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:StateSync-GKR: Design and Evaluation of a Reusable Sparse GKR Engine\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【6†Jinwook Kim】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled StateSync-GKR: Design and Evaluation of a Reusable Sparse GKR Engine, by Jinwook Kim\nL14: \nL15: 【7†View PDF】 【8†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:Authenticated state transitions need proof engines that conserve memory, avoid repeated setup, keep every result bound to its request under parallel execution and return complete proofs at service rates. We present StateSync-GKR, a reusable Rust sumcheck/GKR core with a sparse-Merkle frontend, and evaluate release 1.2.0 against release 1.1 measured on the same day on the same instance types, request corpus and latency budgets.\nL17: Sparse layer reduction keeps additional requested allocation 3,242-fold and peak process memory about 89-fold below the engine's dense reference at width 4,096.\nL18: Release 1.2 changes two parts of the software while proof bytes and verifier acceptance stay fixed: reorganized round arithmetic raises prepared proving throughput 2.4 to 2.7-fold on a 192-core bare-metal host, and a new serving path starts each job as soon as a worker is free, bounds queued work and buffered replies, and returns proofs in completion order bound to request identifiers. On a 192-core VM at 1,000 requests/s, 99th-percentile latency to verified acceptance falls from 440 to 34 ms.\nL19: One connection carries 3,300 requests/s with every request inside one second, confirmed in three 60-second runs and a 30-minute run of 5.94 million requests without a latency or queue trend; at 3,400 requests/s reply traffic reaches the provider's documented 5-Gbit/s single-flow limit, and two connections passed 5,000 requests/s in 15-second screens. In single 15-second screens on 48 cores, the highest passing rate rose from 500 to 2,000/s.\nL20: Offered 1.5 times its highest screened one-connection rate under a memory limit, with excess offers held back by the client, the worker held its resident memory near a fifth of that limit and left no submitted request unresolved. For an integrating service, fewer servers deliver the same proof rate with bounded tail latency, and existing verifiers accept the GKR proofs unchanged.\nL21: Comments:  | 167 pages; 12 main figures and a detailed experimental atlas appendix. v2 evaluates release 1.2.0 (new serving path; reorganized prover arithmetic, byte-identical proofs) against same-day release 1.1 runs on the same instance types. v1 (【5†arXiv:2610.14849v1】) evaluates release 1.1.0. Software: 【9†this https URL†github.com】 (doi:【10†https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23643505†doi.org】)\nL22: Subjects:  | Cryptography and Security (cs.CR); Performance (cs.PF)\nL23: Cite as:  | 【11†arXiv:2610.14849】 [cs.CR]\nL24:    | (or 【12†arXiv:2610.14849v2】 [cs.CR] for this version)\nL25:    | 【13†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2610.14849†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL26: ## Submission history\nL27: \nL28: From: Jinwook Kim [【14†view email】]\nL29: 【5†[v1]】 Sat, 10 Oct 2026 12:06:57 UTC (16,958 KB)\nL30: [v2] Mon, 26 Oct 2026 07:25:00 UTC (10,045 KB)\nL31: \nL32: Full-text links:\nL33: \nL34: ## Access Paper:\nL35: \nL36: View a PDF of the paper titled StateSync-GKR: Design and Evaluation of a Reusable Sparse GKR Engine, by Jinwook Kim\nL37: \nL38:   * 【7†View PDF】\nL39:   * 【8†HTML (experimental)】\nL40:   * 【15†TeX Source】\nL41: \nL42: 【16†view license】\nL43: \nL44: ### Additional Features\nL45: \nL46:   * 【17†Audio Summary】\nL47: ### Current browse context:\nL48: \nL49: cs.CR\nL50: \nL51: 【18†< prev】   |   【19†next >】\nL52: \nL53: 【20†new】 | 【21†recent】 | 【22†2026-10】\nL54: \nL55: Change to browse by:\nL56: \nL57: 【23†cs】\nL58: 【24†cs.PF】\nL59: \nL60: ### References & Citations\nL61: \nL62:   * 【25†SciX†scixplorer.org】\nL63:   * 【26†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】\nL64:   * 【27†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org】\nL65: \nL66: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL67: \nL68: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL69: \nL70: [Button: ×]\nL71: \nL72: loading...\nL73: \nL74: Data provided by:\nL75: \nL76: ### Bookmark\nL77: \nL78: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL79: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL80: \nL81: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL82: \nL83: Bibliographic Explorer (【28†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org】)\nL84: \nL85: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL